I see that `openWorkspaceHome` at line 304-314 calls `navigateReset(url)` without passing any headers (no label/name). So `buildColdStartBreadcrumb` is called with just the URL, and uses the route's title function `(p) => p.workspaceId` which gives the raw ID.

The fix should be in `handleWorkspaceSelect` in `desktop-breadcrumb-bar.tsx` — when selecting a workspace, we have access to the workspace name, so we should pass it. But actually, the real issue is broader: `openWorkspaceHome` doesn't pass a workspace name as a header.

Let me look at how `handleWorkspaceSelect` calls this:

From the earlier read of `desktop-breadcrumb-bar.tsx` (line 119-146):
```tsx
const handleWorkspaceSelect = useCallback(
  (workspaceId: string) => {
    selectWorkspace(workspaceId);
    if (currentSection) { openWorkspaceSection(workspaceId, descriptor.section); return; }
    if (currentArea === "pages") { openWorkspacePages(workspaceId); return; }
    openWorkspaceHome(workspaceId);
  }, ...
);
```

The cleanest fix is to make the route registry's title function smarter — but that would require passing workspace info to the registry. A simpler approach: let `buildColdStartBreadcrumb` annotate workspace breadcrumb items (like it already does for `descriptorId`) and have the `GlobalBreadcrumbShell` or breadcrumb bar resolve the workspace name.

Actually, looking again at the breadcrumb bar — it already has `workspace` prop available. The issue is that `buildColdStartBreadcrumb` creates the stack with the raw workspaceId as label, and then the breadcrumb bar renders that stack directly.

